window.__labLoaded({"id":"6.9","title":"Choosing the Right Test","goal":"Compare incomes (in thousands) in two cities with 12 people each: check the t-test assumptions, then run both the t-test and the Mann-Whitney test and decide which to trust.","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"Enter and look at the data","text":"Skewed incomes in two cities, 12 people each. Always look first: each city has one very large income, which pulls its mean far above its median.","code":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\ncity_a = np.array([33.6, 26.6, 17.6, 64.6, 18.0, 23.9, 23.5, 23.2, 212.7, 39.8, 61.7, 59.1])\ncity_b = np.array([150.9, 42.3, 50.8, 36.2, 65.9, 95.3, 137.2, 45.7, 301.4, 50.7, 88.5, 63.9])\n\nfor name, g in [('A', city_a), ('B', city_b)]:\n    print(f'city {name}: mean = {g.mean():.1f}, median = {np.median(g):.1f}, sd = {g.std(ddof=1):.1f}')\n\nplt.figure(figsize=(6, 3.4))\nplt.boxplot([city_a, city_b], tick_labels=['City A', 'City B'])\nplt.ylabel('income (thousands)')\nplt.show()","out":"city A: mean = 50.4, median = 30.1, sd = 53.9\ncity B: mean = 94.1, median = 64.9, sd = 75.0","figs":["6.9-1.png"]},{"title":"Check normality with Shapiro-Wilk","text":"Shapiro-Wilk asks whether the data look like a sample from a normal curve. A p-value below 0.05 is evidence against normality. With only 12 per group, a normal-looking result would also not prove normality.","code":"for name, g in [('A', city_a), ('B', city_b)]:\n    w = stats.shapiro(g)\n    verdict = 'not normal' if w.pvalue < 0.05 else 'no clear problem'\n    print(f'city {name}: W = {w.statistic:.3f}, p = {w.pvalue:.4f} ({verdict})')","out":"city A: W = 0.601, p = 0.0001 (not normal)\ncity B: W = 0.735, p = 0.0019 (not normal)","figs":[]},{"title":"Check equal spread with Levene","text":"Levene's test asks whether the two groups have the same spread. We use the mean-centred version so it matches SPSS and Stata (SciPy's default centres on the median). A small p-value would mean unequal spreads.","code":"lev = stats.levene(city_a, city_b, center='mean')\n\nprint(f'Levene: statistic = {lev.statistic:.3f}, p = {lev.pvalue:.4f}')\nprint('spreads look equal?', lev.pvalue >= 0.05)","out":"Levene: statistic = 0.931, p = 0.3452\nspreads look equal? True","figs":[]},{"title":"Run the t-test and Mann-Whitney","text":"Welch's t-test compares means and is pulled around by the big incomes. The Mann-Whitney test uses ranks, so extreme values count only as 'the highest'. method='exact' gives an exact p-value when there are no ties.","code":"t_res = stats.ttest_ind(city_a, city_b, equal_var=False)\nu_res = stats.mannwhitneyu(city_a, city_b, method='exact', alternative='two-sided')\n\nprint(f'Welch t-test:   t = {t_res.statistic:.3f}, p = {t_res.pvalue:.4f}')\nprint(f'Mann-Whitney:   U = {u_res.statistic:.1f}, p = {u_res.pvalue:.4f}')","out":"Welch t-test:   t = -1.638, p = 0.1170\nMann-Whitney:   U = 28.0, p = 0.0100","figs":[]},{"title":"Decide which to trust","text":"Both groups failed the normality check and n is small, so the rank-based test is the safer choice here. The two tests disagree at alpha = 0.05, which is a warning that outliers matter. Say which test you chose and why, before looking at p.","code":"alpha = 0.05\nprint('t-test significant?      ', t_res.pvalue <= alpha)\nprint('Mann-Whitney significant?', u_res.pvalue <= alpha)\nprint()\nprint('Small, skewed samples: trust the Mann-Whitney result.')","out":"t-test significant?       False\nMann-Whitney significant? True\n\nSmall, skewed samples: trust the Mann-Whitney result.","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"a <- c(33.6, 26.6, 17.6, 64.6, 18, 23.9, 23.5, 23.2, 212.7, 39.8, 61.7, 59.1)\nb <- c(150.9, 42.3, 50.8, 36.2, 65.9, 95.3, 137.2, 45.7, 301.4, 50.7, 88.5, 63.9)\nshapiro.test(a); shapiro.test(b)\n\nincome <- c(a, b); city <- factor(rep(c(\"A\", \"B\"), each = 12))\ndev <- abs(income - ave(income, city))   # Levene = ANOVA on distances from the group mean\nanova(lm(dev ~ city))\n\nt.test(a, b)          # Welch\nwilcox.test(a, b)     # Mann-Whitney (W equals U)","expect":"Shapiro: A W = 0.6009, p-value = 0.0001092; B W = 0.7352, p-value = 0.001881; Levene F = 0.9306, Pr(>F) = 0.3452; Welch t = -1.6385, df = 19.972, p-value = 0.117; Mann-Whitney W = 28, p-value = 0.01004","note":"Base R has no Levene test, so the ANOVA on distances from each group mean reproduces it (its F equals Levene's statistic). Python's U and R's W are the same number, and both give the exact p-value when there are no ties."},"spss":{"menu":"Analyze > Descriptive Statistics > Explore (Plots > Normality plots with tests); Analyze > Compare Means > Independent-Samples T Test; Analyze > Nonparametric Tests > Legacy Dialogs > 2 Independent Samples","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / city income.\nBEGIN DATA\n1 33.6 1 26.6 1 17.6 1 64.6 1 18 1 23.9\n1 23.5 1 23.2 1 212.7 1 39.8 1 61.7 1 59.1\n2 150.9 2 42.3 2 50.8 2 36.2 2 65.9 2 95.3\n2 137.2 2 45.7 2 301.4 2 50.7 2 88.5 2 63.9\nEND DATA.\nEXAMINE VARIABLES=income BY city\n  /PLOT=NPPLOT.\nT-TEST GROUPS=city(1 2)\n  /VARIABLES=income.\nNPAR TESTS\n  /M-W=income BY city(1 2).","note":"EXAMINE prints the Shapiro-Wilk test per city. The t-test table includes Levene's test (mean-based, matching Python above) and both t rows; read the 'not assumed' row for Welch. In the Mann-Whitney table use the Exact Sig. (2-tailed) line."},"stata":{"code":"clear\nset obs 24\ngenerate city = 1 + (_n > 12)\ngenerate income = real(word(\"33.6 26.6 17.6 64.6 18 23.9 23.5 23.2 212.7 39.8 61.7 59.1 150.9 42.3 50.8 36.2 65.9 95.3 137.2 45.7 301.4 50.7 88.5 63.9\", _n))\nswilk income if city == 1\nswilk income if city == 2\nrobvar income, by(city)\nttest income, by(city) unequal\nranksum income, by(city)","note":"word() picks the nth number from the text so the 24 values fit in one line. robvar prints three versions of Levene's test; use W0 (mean-based). Stata's ttest is pooled unless you add unequal. ranksum reports a z-based p, and an exact p-value too for small samples."},"try":["Change the largest income in city A (212.7) to 40 and predict how the t-test and Mann-Whitney p-values move.","Add 100 to every value in city B (city_b + 100) and predict whether both tests become clearly significant."]});
